 ### Gradient Detachment in Training
-A training strategy where the object field component is detached from the backbone to prevent gradient backpropagation to the rendering part, ensuring independent optimization [S25, unofficial]. With detachment, gradients do not backpropagate to the NeRF backbone; without it, gradients from the object field component backpropagate to the NeRF backbone, causing mutual influence [S25, unofficial]. Detachment achieves better scene rendering and decomposition quality [S25, unofficial]. The hyper-parameter $\Delta d$ for empty 3D point losses is typically 0.05 meters [S25, unofficial].
+A training strategy where the object field component is detached from the backbone to prevent gradient backpropagation to the rendering part, ensuring independent optimization [S25, unofficial]. With detach, the object field component output is detached so gradients do not backpropagate to the NeRF backbone; without detach, gradients from the object field component backpropagate to the NeRF backbone, causing mutual influence [S25, unofficial]. The hyper-parameter $\Delta d$ for empty 3D point losses ($\ell_{3d \text{ empty}} + \ell_{3d \text{ obj}}$) is tested at 0.025, 0.05, 0.10 meters, with 0.05 meters found optimal [S25, unofficial]. With detach achieves better scene rendering and decomposition quality, while without detach causes a significant drop in rendering quality [S25, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot B \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $B$ is the batch size (number of rays), and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(B \cdot K)$ for storing intermediate rendering states during the forward pass, plus $O(\text{model\_size})$ for network weights [S5, S14, unofficial]. VRAM usage is mitigated by chunking rays [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Training on 128x128 images for 250 epochs [S4, unofficial]. On a dataset of 199 images, 5 epochs with batch size 1024 results in ~39,000 iterations [S17, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Larger batch sizes (e.g., $2^{14}$) can hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batch sizes (e.g., $2^{10}$) cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Stochastic sampling allows optimization on large datasets without loading all rays into memory simultaneously [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Blurry predictions if voxel grid resolution is coarse or advanced ray sampling is not used [S17, unofficial]. Slow convergence compared to some accelerated methods [S24].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples})$ per iteration, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (number of rays), and $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S11, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples})$ for storing intermediate rendering states during the forward pass, plus $O(\Theta)$ for model parameters [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. VRAM usage can be mitigated by chunking ray batches [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** In the original NeRF implementation, training on Blender synthetic data (800x800) takes approximately 5 hours on a single GPU for 500,000 iterations [S5, unofficial]. Training on 128x128 images for 250 epochs converges significantly faster [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of training images and scene complexity. Larger batch sizes ($2^{10}$ vs $2^{14}$) trade off data loading performance for GPU occupancy [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, robust convergence on multi-view synthetic data, high-quality novel view synthesis [S5, S11, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence (requires hundreds of thousands of iterations), prone to overfitting on sparse views, struggles with dynamic scenes and transient objects without extensions [S1][S11, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required for static scenes with multi-view input and computational time is not a primary constraint [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** For real-time applications, dynamic scenes, or sparse-view scenarios without additional regularization or architectural modifications [S1][S11, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF training loop was introduced in the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S5, unofficial][S24]. It established the standard procedure of optimizing a coordinate-based MLP using stochastic gradient descent on photometric loss [S5, S14, unofficial]. Early implementations used TensorFlow [S5, unofficial], later ported to PyTorch [S6, S13, unofficial] and JAX [S24]. The method has become the foundational training paradigm for subsequent NeRF variants and related neural rendering techniques [S1, S24].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard protocol for optimizing continuous volumetric representations using coordinate-based MLPs and differentiable volume rendering. The method builds on the emission-absorption model and uses stochastic gradient descent with the Adam optimizer [S5, S11, unofficial]. It has become the foundational training procedure for numerous NeRF variants and extensions [S1][S11, unofficial].
 
